nodecode-windows-updates is a rewrite of Yicheng-Mark's Yicheng-Mark/hermes-windows-plugins:

  https://github.com/Yicheng-Mark/hermes-windows-plugins
  commit 3e46cc3c57288489b7343b706018cd0a8fdeda83
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Yicheng-Mark (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the plugin's `windows-updates` folder, in full. Both PowerShell
scripts are the original's line for line -- the Windows Update Agent's session
interface for the history, `Win32_QuickFixEngineering` for the quick-fix list,
the row count arriving in the environment rather than on the command line --
and so is everything read out of their answers: the result-code table, the
masking of a signed HRESULT to its unsigned `0x` spelling, the 200-character
titles, the 500-entry and 400-row ceilings, and the notes that say what was
left out and why. What changed is the host: the Python plugin ran the scripts
through `subprocess`; here they run through Nodecode's own bounded process
runner, the two tools are boundary contracts, and `/windows-updates` is the
same read in the operator's words. Tests came with it as offline tests against
canned PowerShell output.

Left out: nothing. No part of the original's behaviour was dropped, and the
scripts, being Windows' own interfaces, need no port.
